  "dom_id": "step-q47504",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "The temperature of a certain liquid under heating is given by the function $T(t),$ where $t$ is the time in hours. The values of $T(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| **$t$** | $1.2$ | $1.4$ | $1.6$ | $1.8$ | $2.0$ | $2.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $T(t)$ | $-12$ | $-10$ | $-4$ | $6$ | $10$ | $13$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nDetermine the instantaneous temperature rate at the instant of time $t=1.6$ using a central difference approximation with the step size $h=0.2.$",
  "worked_solution": "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{T}^{\u2032}(t)\u2248\\frac{T(t+h)-T(t-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=1.6$ with step size $h=0.2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{\u2032}(1.6) & \u2248\\frac{T(1.6+0.2)-T(1.6-0.2)}{2(0.2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{T(1.8)-T(1.4)}{2(0.2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-(-10)}{0.4} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{0.4} \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous temperature rate at the instant of time $t=1.6$ is ${40}^{\u2218}$ per hour.",
